{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_hzs6tlgjgszzog2swkwjqegdefdmd264p4tjo5sulzzdwa3d5g5q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_019kisET7wTka6KY8pALheRC","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjj0zf/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"// Rust translation of c_src/src/driver.c (MIT Lincoln Laboratory, 2025).\n//\n// The C library exports exactly four public symbols:\n//     printLine, bad, good, driver\n// All four are reproduced below with the same signatures and the same\n// observable behaviour (including the intentional defect in `bad`).\n\n#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n\nuse std::ffi::c_char;\nuse std::ffi::c_int;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    // Use libc's printf so that stdout buffering / ordering is byte-for-byte\n    // identical to the original C library.\n    fn printf(fmt: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n}\n\n/// Format string `\"%s\\n\"` used by `printLine`.\nstatic FMT_S_NL: [c_char; 4] = [b'%' as c_char, b's' as c_char, b'\\n' as c_char, 0];\n\n/// The string literal `\"string\"` assigned to `data` in `good()`.\nstatic STRING_LITERAL: [c_char; 7] = [\n    b's' as c_char,\n    b't' as c_char,\n    b'r' as c_char,\n    b'i' as c_char,\n    b'n' as c_char,\n    b'g' as c_char,\n    0,\n];\n\n/// ```c\n/// void printLine(const char *line)\n/// {\n///     if (line != NULL)\n///     {\n///         printf(\"%s\\n\", line);\n///     }\n/// }\n/// ```\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printLine(line: *const c_char) {\n    if !line.is_null() {\n        printf(FMT_S_NL.as_ptr(), line);\n    }\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void bad()\n/// {\n///     char *data;\n///     printLine(data);\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// `data` is **never initialised**: the C reads an indeterminate 8-byte stack\n/// slot and passes it straight to `printLine`. This is the intentional defect of\n/// the sample (CWE-457, use of an uninitialised variable) and it is *preserved*,\n/// not fixed.\n///\n/// Because the value read is literally \"whatever the caller left on the stack\",\n/// the observable behaviour of the C is caller-dependent: it prints nothing when\n/// the stale slot happens to be `NULL`, prints stale bytes when it is a readable\n/// pointer, and faults when it is not. Reproducing that faithfully means\n/// reproducing the *frame layout*, so that the Rust reads the exact same address\n/// as the C does. `gcc -O0` compiles the C body to\n///\n/// ```text\n/// push %rbp                  ; rsp -= 8\n/// mov  %rsp,%rbp\n/// sub  $0x10,%rsp\n/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax       ; <-- the indeterminate read, at entry_rsp - 16\n/// mov  %rax,%rdi\n/// call printLine\n/// leave\n/// ret\n/// ```\n///\n/// so the slot is at `entry_rsp - 16`. The naked function below emits exactly\n/// that sequence, which makes the Rust `bad()` read the identical byte range of\n/// the identical stack address for an identical call site — and therefore\n/// produce identical output (or an identical fault).\n///\n/// Stack alignment note: the SysV ABI guarantees `entry_rsp % 16 == 8`, so at the\n/// inner `call` we have `rsp = entry_rsp - 24 ≡ 0 (mod 16)`, exactly as gcc does.\n#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n#[unsafe(naked)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n        \"push rbp\",\n        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n        \"call {print_line}\",\n        \"leave\",\n        \"ret\",\n        print_line = sym printLine,\n    )\n}\n\n/// Portable fallback for targets where the exact `gcc -O0` frame layout cannot\n/// be reproduced. It mirrors the *typical* observed behaviour of the C build\n/// (a non-NULL pointer to an empty string, i.e. a bare newline) because the\n/// value of an indeterminate slot cannot be modelled portably.\n#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n    static GARBAGE: [c_char; 1] = [0];\n    let data: *const c_char = GARBAGE.as_ptr();\n    printLine(data);\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void good()\n/// {\n///     char *data;\n///     data = \"string\";\n///     printLine(data);\n/// }\n/// ```\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn good() {\n    let data: *const c_char = STRING_LITERAL.as_ptr();\n    printLine(data);\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void driver(int useGood)\n/// {\n///     if (useGood) { good(); } else { bad(); }\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// Note the C tests plain truthiness, so *every* non-zero `int` — including\n/// negative values, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX` and any value that would be an\n/// out-of-range enum — selects `good()`. Only exactly `0` selects `bad()`.\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(useGood: c_int) {\n    if useGood != 0 {\n        good();\n    } else {\n        bad();\n    }\n}\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":644,"cache_read_input_tokens":54566,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":644,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e25d0245-0da3-449f-ad2c-b94e24aa7eb9","uuid":"614e0492-e155-439a-a90d-073ec8b7df69","timestamp":"2026-08-28T12:02:01.264Z"}
