{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0183wbRpkyYVWVp5kduJEPSs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of `c_src/src/driver.c` (MIT Lincoln Laboratory, 2025).\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The C library exports exactly four public symbols — `printLine`, `bad`,\n4\t//! `good`, `driver` — and this crate reproduces all four with the same\n5\t//! signatures and the same observable behaviour.\n6\t//!\n7\t//! # Why parts of this are `naked` assembly\n8\t//!\n9\t//! `bad()` in the original is a deliberate CWE-457 defect:\n10\t//!\n11\t//! ```c\n12\t//! void bad() { char *data; printLine(data); }\n13\t//! ```\n14\t//!\n15\t//! `data` is **never assigned**, so the C passes an *indeterminate* 8-byte stack\n16\t//! slot to `printLine`. The value is literally \"whatever the caller left on the\n17\t//! stack at that address\", which means the observable behaviour of the C library\n18\t//! is a function of its own frame layout:\n19\t//!\n20\t//! * if the stale slot happens to be `NULL`, `printLine` prints nothing;\n21\t//! * if it is a readable pointer, the stale bytes are printed;\n22\t//! * otherwise the process takes `SIGSEGV`.\n23\t//!\n24\t//! Empirically (see `tests/configs.rs::cfg_c19_dirty_stack_matrix`) all three\n25\t//! outcomes really do occur for ordinary call sequences, and a \"reasonable\"\n26\t//! translation that substitutes a deterministic empty string diverges from the C\n27\t//! immediately: e.g. after `good(); bad();` the C prints `string`, because\n28\t//! `good`'s frame left the string literal's address in exactly the slot `bad`\n29\t//! reads; and with a dirtied caller stack the C faults where a safe translation\n30\t//! would not.\n31\t//!\n32\t//! Reproducing that byte-for-byte requires reproducing the *stack frame layout*,\n33\t//! so the four bodies below emit the same instruction sequences that\n34\t//! `gcc -O0` (the compiler configuration in `c_src/CMakeLists.txt`, which sets no\n35\t//! optimisation flags) produces for `driver.c`. Concretely this pins:\n36\t//!\n37\t//! * the frame sizes (`push rbp` + `sub rsp, 16` in every function), so the\n38\t//!   indeterminate slot sits at the same address relative to the caller;\n39\t//! * where each function spills its locals, so the *contents* a later\n40\t//!   indeterminate read observes are the same;\n41\t//! * the use of `puts` rather than `printf` — gcc rewrites\n42\t//!   `printf(\"%s\\n\", line)` into `puts(line)`, and the two library routines\n43\t//!   leave different residue on the stack;\n44\t//! * `call` through the PLT rather than a GOT-indirect call.\n45\t//!\n46\t//! Everything else about the crate is ordinary Rust. On any target where this\n47\t//! layout cannot be pinned, `cfg` fallbacks provide the plain, portable\n48\t//! translation instead (documented at each definition).\n49\t\n50\t#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n51\t\n52\tuse std::ffi::c_char;\n53\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n54\t\n55\textern \"C\" {\n56\t    /// `gcc -O0` compiles `printf(\"%s\\n\", line)` in `printLine` into a `puts`\n57\t    /// call, so `puts` is what the C library actually invokes.\n58\t    fn puts(s: *const c_char) -> c_int;\n59\t}\n60\t\n61\t/// The string literal `\"string\"` that `good()` assigns to `data`.\n62\t/// This lives in read-only data, exactly like the C literal.\n63\t#[used]\n64\tstatic STRING_LITERAL: [c_char; 7] = [\n65\t    b's' as c_char,\n66\t    b't' as c_char,\n67\t    b'r' as c_char,\n68\t    b'i' as c_char,\n69\t    b'n' as c_char,\n70\t    b'g' as c_char,\n71\t    0,\n72\t];\n73\t\n74\t// ===========================================================================\n75\t// x86-64 / Linux: layout-exact translation\n76\t// ===========================================================================\n77\t\n78\t/// ```c\n79\t/// void printLine(const char *line)\n80\t/// {\n81\t///     if (line != NULL)\n82\t///     {\n83\t///         printf(\"%s\\n\", line);\n84\t///     }\n85\t/// }\n86\t/// ```\n87\t///\n88\t/// gcc -O0 emits:\n89\t/// ```text\n90\t/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n91\t/// mov  %rdi,-0x8(%rbp)          ; spill the parameter\n92\t/// cmpq $0x0,-0x8(%rbp)          ; if (line != NULL)\n93\t/// je   .Lend\n94\t/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax; mov %rax,%rdi; call puts@plt\n95\t/// .Lend: nop; leave; ret\n96\t/// ```\n97\t#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n98\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n99\t#[unsafe(naked)]\n100\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printLine(_line: *const c_char) {\n101\t    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n102\t        \"push rbp\",\n103\t        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n104\t        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n105\t        \"mov  qword ptr [rbp - 8], rdi\",\n106\t        \"cmp  qword ptr [rbp - 8], 0\",\n107\t        \"je   2f\",\n108\t        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n109\t        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n110\t        \"call {puts}\",\n111\t        \"2:\",\n112\t        \"nop\",\n113\t        \"leave\",\n114\t        \"ret\",\n115\t        puts = sym puts,\n116\t    )\n117\t}\n118\t\n119\t/// ```c\n120\t/// void bad()\n121\t/// {\n122\t///     char *data;\n123\t///     printLine(data);\n124\t/// }\n125\t/// ```\n126\t///\n127\t/// `data` is never initialised. gcc -O0 allocates it at `-0x8(%rbp)` and loads\n128\t/// it without ever storing to it:\n129\t/// ```text\n130\t/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n131\t/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax          ; <-- indeterminate read, at entry_rsp - 16\n132\t/// mov  %rax,%rdi; call printLine@plt\n133\t/// nop; leave; ret\n134\t/// ```\n135\t///\n136\t/// Stack-alignment check: the SysV ABI gives `entry_rsp % 16 == 8`, so at the\n137\t/// inner `call` we have `rsp = entry_rsp - 24 ≡ 0 (mod 16)` — exactly as gcc.\n138\t#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n139\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n140\t#[unsafe(naked)]\n141\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n142\t    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n143\t        \"push rbp\",\n144\t        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n145\t        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n146\t        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n147\t        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n148\t        \"call {print_line}\",\n149\t        \"nop\",\n150\t        \"leave\",\n151\t        \"ret\",\n152\t        print_line = sym printLine,\n153\t    )\n154\t}\n155\t\n156\t/// ```c\n157\t/// void good()\n158\t/// {\n159\t///     char *data;\n160\t///     data = \"string\";\n161\t///     printLine(data);\n162\t/// }\n163\t/// ```\n164\t///\n165\t/// gcc -O0 emits:\n166\t/// ```text\n167\t/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n168\t/// lea  \"string\"(%rip),%rax\n169\t/// mov  %rax,-0x8(%rbp)          ; data = \"string\"\n170\t/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax; mov %rax,%rdi; call printLine@plt\n171\t/// nop; leave; ret\n172\t/// ```\n173\t///\n174\t/// The spill of `data` to `-0x8(%rbp)` matters beyond `good` itself: it is the\n175\t/// very slot a subsequent `bad()` call at the same stack depth reads, which is\n176\t/// why the C prints `string` for the sequence `good(); bad();`.\n177\t#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n178\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n179\t#[unsafe(naked)]\n180\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn good() {\n181\t    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n182\t        \"push rbp\",\n183\t        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n184\t        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n185\t        \"lea  rax, [rip + {lit}]\",\n186\t        \"mov  qword ptr [rbp - 8], rax\",\n187\t        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n188\t        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n189\t        \"call {print_line}\",\n190\t        \"nop\",\n191\t        \"leave\",\n192\t        \"ret\",\n193\t        lit = sym STRING_LITERAL,\n194\t        print_line = sym printLine,\n195\t    )\n196\t}\n197\t\n198\t/// ```c\n199\t/// void driver(int useGood)\n200\t/// {\n201\t///     if (useGood) { good(); } else { bad(); }\n202\t/// }\n203\t/// ```\n204\t///\n205\t/// The C tests plain truthiness, so *every* non-zero `int` — negative values,\n206\t/// `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, and any value that would be an out-of-range enum —\n207\t/// selects `good()`; only exactly `0` selects `bad()`. Note also that the\n208\t/// parameter arrives in `%edi`, so a wider value is truncated to 32 bits before\n209\t/// the test.\n210\t///\n211\t/// gcc -O0 emits:\n212\t/// ```text\n213\t/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n214\t/// mov  %edi,-0x4(%rbp)\n215\t/// cmpl $0x0,-0x4(%rbp)\n216\t/// je   .Lbad\n217\t/// mov  $0x0,%eax; call good@plt\n218\t/// jmp  .Lend\n219\t/// .Lbad: mov $0x0,%eax; call bad@plt\n220\t/// .Lend: nop; leave; ret\n221\t/// ```\n222\t///\n223\t/// The `sub $0x10,%rsp` here is what places `bad`'s indeterminate slot at\n224\t/// `driver_entry_rsp - 40`; a differently sized frame reads different memory and\n225\t/// changes the program's output, so the frame size is part of the contract.\n226\t#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n227\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n228\t#[unsafe(naked)]\n229\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(_useGood: c_int) {\n230\t    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n231\t        \"push rbp\",\n232\t        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n233\t        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n234\t        \"mov  dword ptr [rbp - 4], edi\",\n235\t        \"cmp  dword ptr [rbp - 4], 0\",\n236\t        \"je   2f\",\n237\t        \"mov  eax, 0\",\n238\t        \"call {good}\",\n239\t        \"jmp  3f\",\n240\t        \"2:\",\n241\t        \"mov  eax, 0\",\n242\t        \"call {bad}\",\n243\t        \"3:\",\n244\t        \"nop\",\n245\t        \"leave\",\n246\t        \"ret\",\n247\t        good = sym good,\n248\t        bad = sym bad,\n249\t    )\n250\t}\n251\t\n252\t// ===========================================================================\n253\t// Portable fallback (non x86-64-Linux targets)\n254\t// ===========================================================================\n255\t//\n256\t// Here the exact frame layout of `gcc -O0` cannot be pinned, so the\n257\t// indeterminate read in `bad()` cannot be modelled. These definitions implement\n258\t// the C's *specified* behaviour and, for `bad()`, the most common outcome\n259\t// observed from the reference build (a non-NULL pointer to an empty string, i.e.\n260\t// a bare newline).\n261\t\n262\t/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n263\t#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n264\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n265\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printLine(line: *const c_char) {\n266\t    if !line.is_null() {\n267\t        puts(line);\n268\t    }\n269\t}\n270\t\n271\t/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n272\t#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n273\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n274\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n275\t    static GARBAGE: [c_char; 1] = [0];\n276\t    printLine(GARBAGE.as_ptr());\n277\t}\n278\t\n279\t/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n280\t#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n281\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n282\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn good() {\n283\t    printLine(STRING_LITERAL.as_ptr());\n284\t}\n285\t\n286\t/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n287\t#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n288\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n289\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(useGood: c_int) {\n290\t    if useGood != 0 {\n291\t        good();\n292\t    } else {\n293\t        bad();\n294\t    }\n295\t}\n296\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e25d0245-0da3-449f-ad2c-b94e24aa7eb9","uuid":"074138da-8c52-4269-94f2-5a23b06068d8","timestamp":"2026-08-28T12:28:54.980Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjj0zf/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Rust translation of `c_src/src/driver.c` (MIT Lincoln Laboratory, 2025).\n//!\n//! The C library exports exactly four public symbols — `printLine`, `bad`,\n//! `good`, `driver` — and this crate reproduces all four with the same\n//! signatures and the same observable behaviour.\n//!\n//! # Why parts of this are `naked` assembly\n//!\n//! `bad()` in the original is a deliberate CWE-457 defect:\n//!\n//! ```c\n//! void bad() { char *data; printLine(data); }\n//! ```\n//!\n//! `data` is **never assigned**, so the C passes an *indeterminate* 8-byte stack\n//! slot to `printLine`. The value is literally \"whatever the caller left on the\n//! stack at that address\", which means the observable behaviour of the C library\n//! is a function of its own frame layout:\n//!\n//! * if the stale slot happens to be `NULL`, `printLine` prints nothing;\n//! * if it is a readable pointer, the stale bytes are printed;\n//! * otherwise the process takes `SIGSEGV`.\n//!\n//! Empirically (see `tests/configs.rs::cfg_c19_dirty_stack_matrix`) all three\n//! outcomes really do occur for ordinary call sequences, and a \"reasonable\"\n//! translation that substitutes a deterministic empty string diverges from the C\n//! immediately: e.g. after `good(); bad();` the C prints `string`, because\n//! `good`'s frame left the string literal's address in exactly the slot `bad`\n//! reads; and with a dirtied caller stack the C faults where a safe translation\n//! would not.\n//!\n//! Reproducing that byte-for-byte requires reproducing the *stack frame layout*,\n//! so the four bodies below emit the same instruction sequences that\n//! `gcc -O0` (the compiler configuration in `c_src/CMakeLists.txt`, which sets no\n//! optimisation flags) produces for `driver.c`. Concretely this pins:\n//!\n//! * the frame sizes (`push rbp` + `sub rsp, 16` in every function), so the\n//!   indeterminate slot sits at the same address relative to the caller;\n//! * where each function spills its locals, so the *contents* a later\n//!   indeterminate read observes are the same;\n//! * the use of `puts` rather than `printf` — gcc rewrites\n//!   `printf(\"%s\\n\", line)` into `puts(line)`, and the two library routines\n//!   leave different residue on the stack;\n//! * `call` through the PLT rather than a GOT-indirect call.\n//!\n//! Everything else about the crate is ordinary Rust. On any target where this\n//! layout cannot be pinned, `cfg` fallbacks provide the plain, portable\n//! translation instead (documented at each definition).\n\n#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n\nuse std::ffi::c_char;\nuse std::ffi::c_int;\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    /// `gcc -O0` compiles `printf(\"%s\\n\", line)` in `printLine` into a `puts`\n    /// call, so `puts` is what the C library actually invokes.\n    fn puts(s: *const c_char) -> c_int;\n}\n\n/// The string literal `\"string\"` that `good()` assigns to `data`.\n/// This lives in read-only data, exactly like the C literal.\n#[used]\nstatic STRING_LITERAL: [c_char; 7] = [\n    b's' as c_char,\n    b't' as c_char,\n    b'r' as c_char,\n    b'i' as c_char,\n    b'n' as c_char,\n    b'g' as c_char,\n    0,\n];\n\n// ===========================================================================\n// x86-64 / Linux: layout-exact translation\n// ===========================================================================\n\n/// ```c\n/// void printLine(const char *line)\n/// {\n///     if (line != NULL)\n///     {\n///         printf(\"%s\\n\", line);\n///     }\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// gcc -O0 emits:\n/// ```text\n/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n/// mov  %rdi,-0x8(%rbp)          ; spill the parameter\n/// cmpq $0x0,-0x8(%rbp)          ; if (line != NULL)\n/// je   .Lend\n/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax; mov %rax,%rdi; call puts@plt\n/// .Lend: nop; leave; ret\n/// ```\n#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n#[unsafe(naked)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printLine(_line: *const c_char) {\n    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n        \"push rbp\",\n        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n        \"mov  qword ptr [rbp - 8], rdi\",\n        \"cmp  qword ptr [rbp - 8], 0\",\n        \"je   2f\",\n        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n        \"call {puts}\",\n        \"2:\",\n        \"nop\",\n        \"leave\",\n        \"ret\",\n        puts = sym puts,\n    )\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void bad()\n/// {\n///     char *data;\n///     printLine(data);\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// `data` is never initialised. gcc -O0 allocates it at `-0x8(%rbp)` and loads\n/// it without ever storing to it:\n/// ```text\n/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax          ; <-- indeterminate read, at entry_rsp - 16\n/// mov  %rax,%rdi; call printLine@plt\n/// nop; leave; ret\n/// ```\n///\n/// Stack-alignment check: the SysV ABI gives `entry_rsp % 16 == 8`, so at the\n/// inner `call` we have `rsp = entry_rsp - 24 ≡ 0 (mod 16)` — exactly as gcc.\n#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n#[unsafe(naked)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n        \"push rbp\",\n        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n        \"call {print_line}\",\n        \"nop\",\n        \"leave\",\n        \"ret\",\n        print_line = sym printLine,\n    )\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void good()\n/// {\n///     char *data;\n///     data = \"string\";\n///     printLine(data);\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// gcc -O0 emits:\n/// ```text\n/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n/// lea  \"string\"(%rip),%rax\n/// mov  %rax,-0x8(%rbp)          ; data = \"string\"\n/// mov  -0x8(%rbp),%rax; mov %rax,%rdi; call printLine@plt\n/// nop; leave; ret\n/// ```\n///\n/// The spill of `data` to `-0x8(%rbp)` matters beyond `good` itself: it is the\n/// very slot a subsequent `bad()` call at the same stack depth reads, which is\n/// why the C prints `string` for the sequence `good(); bad();`.\n#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n#[unsafe(naked)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn good() {\n    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n        \"push rbp\",\n        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n        \"lea  rax, [rip + {lit}]\",\n        \"mov  qword ptr [rbp - 8], rax\",\n        \"mov  rax, qword ptr [rbp - 8]\",\n        \"mov  rdi, rax\",\n        \"call {print_line}\",\n        \"nop\",\n        \"leave\",\n        \"ret\",\n        lit = sym STRING_LITERAL,\n        print_line = sym printLine,\n    )\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void driver(int useGood)\n/// {\n///     if (useGood) { good(); } else { bad(); }\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// The C tests plain truthiness, so *every* non-zero `int` — negative values,\n/// `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, and any value that would be an out-of-range enum —\n/// selects `good()`; only exactly `0` selects `bad()`. Note also that the\n/// parameter arrives in `%edi`, so a wider value is truncated to 32 bits before\n/// the test.\n///\n/// gcc -O0 emits:\n/// ```text\n/// push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; sub $0x10,%rsp\n/// mov  %edi,-0x4(%rbp)\n/// cmpl $0x0,-0x4(%rbp)\n/// je   .Lbad\n/// mov  $0x0,%eax; call good@plt\n/// jmp  .Lend\n/// .Lbad: mov $0x0,%eax; call bad@plt\n/// .Lend: nop; leave; ret\n/// ```\n///\n/// The `sub $0x10,%rsp` here is what places `bad`'s indeterminate slot at\n/// `driver_entry_rsp - 40`; a differently sized frame reads different memory and\n/// changes the program's output, so the frame size is part of the contract.\n#[cfg(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\"))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n#[unsafe(naked)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(_useGood: c_int) {\n    core::arch::naked_asm!(\n        \"push rbp\",\n        \"mov  rbp, rsp\",\n        \"sub  rsp, 16\",\n        \"mov  dword ptr [rbp - 4], edi\",\n        \"cmp  dword ptr [rbp - 4], 0\",\n        \"je   2f\",\n        \"mov  eax, 0\",\n        \"call {good}\",\n        \"jmp  3f\",\n        \"2:\",\n        \"mov  eax, 0\",\n        \"call {bad}\",\n        \"3:\",\n        \"nop\",\n        \"leave\",\n        \"ret\",\n        good = sym good,\n        bad = sym bad,\n    )\n}\n\n// ===========================================================================\n// Portable fallback (non x86-64-Linux targets)\n// ===========================================================================\n//\n// Here the exact frame layout of `gcc -O0` cannot be pinned, so the\n// indeterminate read in `bad()` cannot be modelled. These definitions implement\n// the C's *specified* behaviour and, for `bad()`, the most common outcome\n// observed from the reference build (a non-NULL pointer to an empty string, i.e.\n// a bare newline).\n\n/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printLine(line: *const c_char) {\n    if !line.is_null() {\n        puts(line);\n    }\n}\n\n/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n    static GARBAGE: [c_char; 1] = [0];\n    printLine(GARBAGE.as_ptr());\n}\n\n/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn good() {\n    printLine(STRING_LITERAL.as_ptr());\n}\n\n/// See the x86-64 definition for documentation.\n#[cfg(not(all(target_arch = \"x86_64\", target_os = \"linux\")))]\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn driver(useGood: c_int) {\n    if useGood != 0 {\n        good();\n    } else {\n        bad();\n    }\n}\n","numLines":296,"startLine":1,"totalLines":296}}}
